----------------------------------
- Classes and Objects by Analogy -
----------------------------------

Java is a class-based, object oriented programming language. In such a language, we design and write classes to describe 
types of objects. For each type of object involved in the solution to the problem we are trying to solve, we design and 
write a class. If we are trying to solve the problem of keeping track of and managing bank accounts for bank customers, 
we would probably design and write a customer class, and a bank account class, and so on. The classes describe the 
attributes (the things it knows, or stores, or keeps track of), and the operations (the things it does) for each type of 
object.


**

	A class defines a type of object - its attributes (the things it knows), and its operations (the things it does).

**

A class defines a type of object - its attributes (things it knows), and its operations (things it does).

Here is an analogy. Classes are like cookie cutters, and objects are like cookies. If you wanted to make a bunch of 
Christmas tree shaped cookies, you could shape each cookie by hand. This would be a lot of work, and each cookie would 
be a little different - because we are making them by hand one at a time. On the other hand, if we have a Christmas tree 
shaped cookie cutter, we could use it to quickly and easily cut out many identical cookies.

Here is another analogy. Classes are like blueprints, and objects are like buildings. If you wanted to make a bunch of 
houses, you could just build one house, and then try to build another house just like the first - building one after 
another, by sawing, and hammering, and painting, and so on. You could try to make each house look just like all the 
others, but this would be a lot of work, slow, and error prone. Usually, an architect designs a home and creates the 
blueprint that describes the house in detail. Then the builders can use this blueprint to build any number of identical 
homes, more quickly and with fewer errors.

A class is like a blueprint that the computer can use to build an object in the computer's memory. The class (blueprint) 
describes the object in detail, including all of the attributes (the things it knows, or stores, or keeps track of) and 
operations (the things it does). This one class (blueprint) can be used to build any number of identical objects in 
memory.

A class is like a cookie cutter that the computer can use to make objects (cookies) in the computer's memory. 
This one class (cookie cutter) can be used to make any number of identical objects (cookies) in memory.
